To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `IHttpContextAccessor` and the `JwtSecurityTokenHandler`. Here's an example of how to do this:

1. Inject the `IHttpContextAccessor` into your controller constructor:
```csharp
private readonly IHttpContextAccessor _httpContextAccessor;

public MyController(IHttpContextAccessor httpContextAccessor)
{
    _httpContextAccessor = httpContextAccessor;
}
```
1. In your action method, get the `HttpContext` from the `_httpContextAccessor`:
```csharp
var context = _httpContextAccessor.HttpContext;
```
1. Get the `JwtSecurityTokenHandler` from the `context.Request.Headers`:
```csharp
var jwtSecurityTokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
```
1. Extract the username from the JWT token using the `jwtSecurityTokenHandler.ReadJwtToken()` method:
```csharp
var token = context.Request.Headers["Authorization"].ToString().Substring("Bearer ".Length);
var userName = jwtSecurityTokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token).Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "name")?.Value;
```
1. Use the `userName` variable to perform any actions you need to do with the username.

Here's an example of how to put it all together in a simple action method:
```csharp
[Route("api/[controller]")]
[HttpGet]
public string GetUserName()
{
    var context = _httpContextAccessor.HttpContext;
    var jwtSecurityTokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
    var token = context.Request.Headers["Authorization"].ToString().Substring("Bearer ".Length);
    var userName = jwtSecurityTokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token).Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "name")?.Value;

    return $"Hello, {userName}!";
}
```
