Here's a simple React utility that removes widows (single-word lines) from text. You can use this component by wrapping your text content inside it:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ children }) => {
  const [text, setText] = useState(children);

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    setText(event.target.value);
  };

  const removeWidows = () => {
    setText(text.replace(/(\w+)\s+$/g, '$1'));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={text} onChange={handleChange} />
      <button onClick={removeWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
      <p>{text}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

This component takes the `children` prop, which represents the text content you want to remove widows from. It then creates a state variable `text` and updates it whenever the text area is changed. The `removeWidows` function replaces any single-word lines with just the word itself, and the updated text is displayed below the text area.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows. This component will wrap your content and adjust the line breaks to prevent widows from occurring.

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const WidowRemover = ({ children }) => {
  const [lines, setLines] = useState([]);

  const handleTextChange = (event) => {
    const text = event.target.value;
    const linesArray = text.split('\n');
    setLines(linesArray);
  };

  const renderLines = () => {
    return lines.map((line, index) => (
      <div key={index}>
        {line}
        <br />
      </div>
    ));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={lines.join('\n')} onChange={handleTextChange} />
      {renderLines()}
    </div>
  );
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

To use this component, simply wrap your content with it:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import WidowRemover from './WidowRemover';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <WidowRemover>
        <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.</p>
        <p>Sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua.</p>
        <p>Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat.</p>
      </WidowRemover>
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This component will automatically adjust the line breaks to prevent widows from occurring. You can also customize the text area's height and other properties as needed.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows from your text:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [formattedText, setFormattedText] = useState(text);

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const words = formattedText.split(' ');
    const lastLine = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
      const word = words[i];
      const line = lastLine.join(' ') + ' ' + word;

      if (line.trim() !== '') {
        lastLine.push(word);
      } else {
        lastLine = [word];
      }
    }

    setFormattedText(lastLine.join(' '));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{formattedText}</p>
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the text you want to format and it will remove any widows:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  const text = 'This is a sample text with some widows. This is another sentence.';

  return (
    <div>
      <RemoveWidows text={text} />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

